<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Galilee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Galilee is a region in the north of present-day Israel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> in 1187, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-galilee">Sea of Galilee</a>, was the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>'s reconquest, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in 1260 in Galilee set a lasting western frontier for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/galilee.jpg" alt="Galilee" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Baránek; derivative work by TheCuriousGnome · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lower_Galilee_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tiglath-pileser III's deportations from <b>Galilee</b> in 732 BCE and the fall of Samaria in 722 BCE, followed by Sargon II's deportation of the population of the Kingdom of Israel, the &quot;Ten Lost Tribes&quot; of biblical tradition, profoundly marked the history of Israel and Judah; the Assyrian crisis is…</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol expansion met a decisive limit, however, at the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1260, when the Mamluk army under Sultan Qutuz and the general Baybars defeated a Mongol force commanded by Kitbuqa in <b>Galilee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Israel held the more fertile and populous part of the southern Levant: the hill country of Samaria, the Jezreel Valley, <b>Galilee</b> and the lands of Gilead east of the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The great lordships, among them <b>Galilee</b>, Jaffa and Ascalon, Sidon and Oultrejordain, held extensive rights over their lands and subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ottoman times, mostly Arab clans and local sheikhs; Jews owned 5.23 per cent of the territory in 1945 and, by Stein's estimate, 7.51 per cent in May 1948, mostly in <b>Galilee</b> and the coastal plain, while Arab ownership of arable land averaged 68 per cent of a district.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Christianity began with Jesus of Nazareth, a Jewish teacher from <b>Galilee</b> who lived under Roman rule.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
